//! # What the verdict carries
//!
//! The person who has to resume a stopped run should not have to dig through the
//! transcript to find out what the run was stuck on, so a verdict names the call: the
//! tool, the arguments (which file, which query), where in the run it happened, and --
//! when the answer repeated -- whether the output cap had cut that answer short. Point
//! 1 of #35, in the terms the thread used.
//!
//! Two shapes are deliberately *not* loops:
//!
//! * **A capped answer is still a loop when the call is identical.** The same arguments
//!   cannot return the part the cap cut, so re-reading is not recovery -- but the
//!   verdict says the answer was capped, because that is why the model kept coming
//!   back and the thing a resume instruction has to change: a narrower range, not the
//!   same file again.
//! * **A re-read after a compaction is recovery.** Compaction replaces the messages
//!   the model had read with a summary, so reading the same file again restores what
//!   the session lost. [`LoopTracker::reset_after_compaction`] is called at that
//!   point, and without it the guard would abort a run that was doing the right thing
//!   (#35, point 4).
//!
//! Counting is over *consecutive* calls: any other call, or the same call returning
//! different bytes, starts the count again. That is what keeps the four-in-a-row
//! incident apart from a healthy run that revisits a file after a detour, and it is
//! why the threshold can stay at [`MAX_IDENTICAL_ANSWERS`].
